Notation — mathematics and code, inline and displayed.

<section #l1-inline-math | `<inline-math>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `math` · Layer 1 `<inline-math>` · JATS `<inline-formula>` · `content` `opaque`

*No kwargs or boolean flags.*

Produced by: canonical `<inline-math>` · sigil `<$ … $>`.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code #code:l1-inline-math-1>
The identity <$ a^2 + b^2 = c^2 $> holds for right triangles.
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-inline-math-1>
The identity <$ a^2 + b^2 = c^2 $> holds for right triangles.
</minipage>

*The `$` sigil. Opaque LaTeX content rendered inline by KaTeX and wrapped in &lt;inline-math&gt; for CSS targeting. The sigil carries no attributes — id and classes are not supported for inline math.*

</section>

<section #l1-display-math | `<display-math>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `math` · Layer 1 `<display-math>` · JATS `<disp-formula>` · `content` `opaque`

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `+numbered` | boolean | default `true` | Whether this equation participates in the document-wide numbered sequence. Use +numbered (default) to number, -numbered to suppress. Can also be written as numbered=true / numbered=false. When suppressed, the equation renders without a number and is not added to the numbered counter. The config key number-equations=false suppresses all equations unless overridden per-element with +numbered. |

Produced by: canonical `<display-math>` · sigil `<$$ … $$>`.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code #code:l1-display-math-1>
<$$ \sum_{i=1}^{n} x_i = X $$>
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-display-math-1>
<$$ \sum_{i=1}^{n} x_i = X $$>
</minipage>

*The `$$` sigil. Display-mode LaTeX rendered by KaTeX on its own line; numbered by default (the equation number is appended after the KaTeX output).*

</section>

<section #l1-math | `<math>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `math` · Layer 1 `<math>` · JATS `<disp-formula>` · `content` `opaque`

*No kwargs or boolean flags.*

Produced by: canonical `<math>`.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code #code:l1-math-1>
<math>
E = mc^2
</math>
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-math-1>
<math>
E = mc^2
</math>
</minipage>

*Long-form `&lt;math&gt;` block. Semantically equivalent to the `&lt;$$ E = mc^2 $$&gt;` display-math sigil — both render block-level LaTeX math via KaTeX. Use the long-form when the source is multi-line or when explicit tag bounds aid readability; use the sigil for brevity.*

</section>

<section #l1-align | `<align>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `math` · Layer 1 `<align>` · JATS `<disp-formula>` · `content` `opaque`

*No kwargs or boolean flags.*

Produced by: canonical `<align>`.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code #code:l1-align-1>
<align>
x^2 + y^2 &= z^2 \\
a + b &= c
</align>
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-align-1>
<align>
x^2 + y^2 &= z^2 \\
a + b &= c
</align>
</minipage>

*Two aligned equations. The `&` marks the alignment column (here, the `=` sign). Handler wraps in `\begin{aligned}...\end{aligned}` (KaTeX-compatible variant of LaTeX's `align`).*

</section>

<section #l1-eqnarray | `<eqnarray>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `math` · Layer 1 `<eqnarray>` · JATS `<disp-formula>` · `content` `opaque`

*No kwargs or boolean flags.*

Produced by: canonical `<eqnarray>`.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code #code:l1-eqnarray-1>
<eqnarray>
f(x) &=& x^2 \\
g(x) &=& 2x
</eqnarray>
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-eqnarray-1>
<eqnarray>
f(x) &=& x^2 \\
g(x) &=& 2x
</eqnarray>
</minipage>

*Two equations rendered via KaTeX's `aligned` env (the supported equivalent of LaTeX's `eqnarray`).*

</section>

<section #l1-cases | `<cases>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `math` · Layer 1 `<cases>` · JATS `<disp-formula>` · `content` `opaque`

*No kwargs or boolean flags.*

Produced by: canonical `<cases>`.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code #code:l1-cases-1>
<cases>
x^2 & \text{if } x \ge 0 \\
-x^2 & \text{if } x < 0
</cases>
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-cases-1>
<cases>
x^2 & \text{if } x \ge 0 \\
-x^2 & \text{if } x < 0
</cases>
</minipage>

*A two-case piecewise definition. Handler wraps in `\begin{cases}...\end{cases}`.*

</section>

<section #l1-matrix | `<matrix>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `math` · Layer 1 `<matrix>` · JATS `<disp-formula>` · `content` `opaque`

*No kwargs or boolean flags.*

Produced by: canonical `<matrix>`.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code #code:l1-matrix-1>
<matrix>
1 & 2 \\
3 & 4
</matrix>
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-matrix-1>
<matrix>
1 & 2 \\
3 & 4
</matrix>
</minipage>

*A 2×2 matrix. The handler wraps the body in `\begin{matrix}...\end{matrix}` before KaTeX renders.*

</section>

<section #l1-inline-code | `<inline-code>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `code` · Layer 1 `<inline-code>` · JATS `<monospace>` · `content` `opaque`

*No kwargs or boolean flags.*

Produced by: canonical `<inline-code>` · sigil `<` … `>` · markdown ``…``.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code #code:l1-inline-code-1>
Assign with <` x = 1 `> at the top of the function.
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-inline-code-1>
Assign with <` x = 1 `> at the top of the function.
</minipage>

*The single-backtick sigil. With no pipe, the whole body is opaque code content rendered as &lt;code&gt; (the same output as a markdown backtick span).*

<code #code:l1-inline-code-2>
Call <` python | factorial(n) `> to recurse.
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-inline-code-2>
Call <` python | factorial(n) `> to recurse.
</minipage>

*The first positional token before the pipe is the language, emitted as a `language-X` class on the &lt;code&gt; (discoverable by highlighters; the interpreter applies no highlighting itself).*

</section>

<section #l1-code | `<code>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `code` · Layer 1 `<code>` · JATS `<monospace>` · `content` `opaque` → `<text-content>`

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `language` | kwarg |  | The programming language of the code. Maps to a class like "language-python" for syntax highlighting via shiki/prism. |

Produced by: canonical `<code>`.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code #code:l1-code-1>
The function is `factorial`.
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-code-1>
The function is `factorial`.
</minipage>

*Plain markdown backticks produce inline &lt;code&gt;. The most common authoring path.*

<code #code:l1-code-2>
Use `<`code`>` for inline code.
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-code-2>
Use `<`code`>` for inline code.
</minipage>

*The enscribe sigil form. Equivalent to plain markdown backticks but supports attributes.*

<code #code:l1-code-3>
<code language=python | def hello(): print("hi")>
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-code-3>
<code language=python | def hello(): print("hi")>
</minipage>

<code #code:l1-code-4>
<code #factorial-impl language=python | def factorial(n):>
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-code-4>
<code #factorial-impl language=python | def factorial(n):>
</minipage>

</section>

<section #l1-code-block | `<code-block>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `code` · Layer 1 `<code-block>` · JATS `<code>` · `content` `opaque`

*No kwargs or boolean flags.*

Produced by: canonical `<code-block>` · sigil `<``` … ``` >` · markdown ````…````.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code #code:l1-code-block-1>
<``` python | print("hello, world") ```>
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-code-block-1>
<``` python | print("hello, world") ```>
</minipage>

*The triple-backtick sigil. The first positional token is the language (emitted as a `language-X` class on the &lt;code&gt;); the pipe separates it from the verbatim, newline-preserving content.*

<code #code:l1-code-block-2>
<``` this is all content ```>
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-code-block-2>
<``` this is all content ```>
</minipage>

*The no-pipe form: the entire body is opaque content with no language extraction.*

</section>
